 "drivingQuestion": "What does 'anything that is permanent' come to include?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "The clause includes R' Ḥanina's materials; R' Ḥiyya adds more",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "gittin.19a.7",
   "daf": "19a",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "וּבְכׇל דָּבָר שֶׁהוּא מִתְקַיֵּים וְכוּ׳: לְאֵתוֹיֵי מַאי? לְאֵתוֹיֵי הָא דְתָנֵי רַבִּי חֲנִינָא: כְּתָבוֹ בְּמֵי טַרְיָא וְאַפְצָא – כָּשֵׁר. תָּנֵי רַבִּי חִיָּיא: כְּתָבוֹ בַּאֲבָר, בִּשְׁחוֹר וּבְשִׁיחוֹר – כָּשֵׁר.",
   "en": "The mishna taught that one is allowed to write a bill of divorce «with anything» that produces «permanent» writing. The Gemara asks: This statement is meant «to add what?» The Gemara answers: «To add what Rabbi Ḥanina taught:» If «he wrote it with mei teriya or» water in which «gallnuts [aftza]» were soaked, then it is «valid,» as the writing is permanent. «Rabbi Ḥiyya teaches:» If «he wrote it with lead, with coals, or with black paint» then it is «valid.»"
  }
 ],
